Subject: [PATCH] Switch 2 titles (experimental): a game folder's ExeFS and
 RomFS, with eden-nx2's session settings

From Oliv3rmoon's Nightshade (79f2ba0, 02fbd2e), onto this fork's own code:

- A game folder that holds exefs/ (main, main.npdm) and romfs/ is a game, by the path of its main
  (exefs_game.h): its title ID from main.npdm, its name from a control.nacp beside it, and a
  Switch 2 title is one whose npdm has the Switch 2 layout (eden_game_is_switch2). The last and
  recent games keep such a path; the update and DLC scan skips ExeFS and RomFS folders.
- A Switch 2 session starts with eden-nx2's run settings (12 GiB layout, lowest GPU accuracy, no
  reactive flushing, ASTC decoded on the GPU, split GPU thread); "switch2" in the settings file
  changes three of them, for every Switch 2 title or one (docs/SETTINGS.md). The Turing macro
  compiler stays off on the console, which refuses its executable memory. eden-nx2's process-wide
  Switch 2 state is put back before every session (nx2_session.h), and the split GPU thread's mode
  is read once per session. Two optimizations eden-nx2 measured only with Switch 2 titles stay off
  for Switch 1 games (tools/eden-patches/12).
- Six emulated cores: eden-nx2 raises NUM_CPU_CORES from 4 to 6 for every title. Everything
  performance.h counts per core is sized by kGuestCores, and the derived physical_core.cpp asserts
  it: with four places the JIT callbacks, CPU write statistics and CPU samples of cores 4 and 5 were
  written past their arrays (and SampleCpu's at() threw).
- ASTC transcoding keeps half the hardware threads, as Switch 1 games were measured with.

Checked: every derivation finds its anchor on eden-nx2 (PS5 build with graphics, Linux host);
metadata_bridge.cpp, main.cpp and performance.cpp compile against eden-nx2's headers; the settings
store's check passes with the Switch 2 settings and paths. Not run on a console.

